{# ===========================================================================
   master_resume.html.j2 — the document SHELL for master/upgrad_resume.html.

   ⛔ SCAFFOLDING LIVES HERE. CONTENT COMES FROM THE DATABASE.
   Nothing in this file may state a fact about his career. If a name, a date, a
   number or a bullet appears below as a literal, it is in the wrong place —
   it belongs in resume_documents / resume_roles / resume_education /
   resume_profile / resume_sections / resume_blocks (db/migrations/012).

   ⛔ THE OUTPUT MUST BE BYTE-IDENTICAL to the committed master. Four rendering
   settings are load-bearing and `automation/resume_db.py` sets all four:

     autoescape=False        every value here is verbatim source HTML. With
                             autoescape on, {{ b.html }} renders &lt;strong&gt;
                             and &amp; doubles to &amp;amp; — all 91 paragraphs
                             corrupted at once, invisibly until an exported PDF
                             has no bold in it.
     keep_trailing_newline   Jinja strips the final newline by default. The file
                             ends "</html>\n"; one byte fails the gate.
     trim_blocks             a line holding only {% … %} must vanish, not leave
     lstrip_blocks           a blank line behind it.

   Plain-text values from the database (a company, a title, a location, an
   education line, a field label) pass through the `h` filter, which escapes
   & < > and NOTHING else — McDonald's keeps its apostrophe, "Co-Founder & VP"
   comes back as "Co-Founder &amp; VP". Verbatim-HTML values (bullets, headings,
   the intro paragraph, the profile values) are emitted raw, because the database
   holds them in the exact spelling the file uses.

   Whitespace is part of the contract. Every section is:

       <h2>N. Heading</h2>                     column 0, heading BEFORE its div
       <div class="section copy-target" id="…"> column 0
         <button …>Copy</button>                two spaces
         <p>…</p>                               two spaces, one line per bullet
       </div>                                   column 0
       (exactly one blank line, after the last section too)
   =========================================================================== #}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ doc.lang }}">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <title>{{ doc.title|h }}</title>
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{ doc.favicon_href }}" />
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ doc.stylesheet_href }}" />
</head>
<body>
<main class="page" id="main">

<!--{{ doc.contract_comment }}-->

<h1>{{ doc.title|h }}</h1>
<p>{{ doc.intro_html }}</p>

{# --- The dates table. CARD-ONLY: what reaches an exported PDF lives in the
       Hiration card, which the bot never writes. This table is the assertion,
       and the <strong> on the current role's end date is the file shouting the
       one date the export checklist insists must be right. --------------- #}
<h2>{{ doc.dates_heading|h }}</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Employer</th><th>Title</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Location</th></tr>
{% for r in roles %}
<tr><td>{{ r.n }}</td><td>{{ r.company|h }}</td><td>{{ r.role_title|h }}</td><td>{{ r.date_from|h }}</td><td>{% if r.date_to_emphasised %}<strong>{{ r.date_to|h }}</strong>{% else %}{{ r.date_to|h }}{% endif %}</td><td>{{ r.location|h }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
</table>
{# --- Education. A CLAUDE.md fixed fact: "MS in Computer Science, University of
       Houston. Never downgrade it." The note after it is an instruction to
       whoever reads this file, not résumé text, and is stored separately. --- #}
<p><strong>{{ doc.education_label|h }}:</strong> {{ education_lines|join('\n') }}
{{ doc.education_note_html }}</p>

<h2>{{ doc.personal_heading|h }}</h2>
<p>{{ doc.personal_note_html }}</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr>
{% for p in profile %}
<tr><td>{{ p.field_label|h }}</td><td>{{ p.value_html }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
</table>

{# --- The fifteen sections. The ten `quick-*` ids are a parser contract:
       upgrad_apply.py parses by id and a misspelt one is SKIPPED SILENTLY while
       the export still reports success. resume_db.py asserts all ten are present
       and non-empty before this template is rendered.

       Every bullet is a standalone <p>, never a <ul><li>: upGrad's paste
       sanitiser strips bold from list-rooted content. Emitting <p>
       unconditionally turns that rule from documentation into a property of the
       generator.

       data-copy-html="1" is mandatory on every button — without it a copied
       selection loses its bold, and that does not surface until the PDF. --- #}
{% for s in sections %}
<h2>{{ s.heading }}</h2>
<div class="section copy-target" id="{{ s.section_id }}">
  <button class="copy-btn" data-copy-target="#{{ s.section_id }}" data-copy-html="1">Copy</button>
{% for b in s.blocks %}
  <p>{{ b.html }}</p>
{% endfor %}
</div>

{% endfor %}
</main>
<script src="{{ doc.script_src }}" defer></script>
</body>
</html>
